// @error return expects a value, got void
// CONTRACT (must-error): the same rule under an explicit `: void`, which is the one DECLARED
// return type the mismatch check cannot catch a void return with — `assignable(void, void)`
// holds, so `return <void call>` passed every assignability rule and reached codegen, which
// emitted a `return` with nothing on the stack.
//
// This position is the counter-example to "the annotated paths were already covered": the
// annotation is present and written, and it is precisely the annotation AGREEING with the
// void that let it through. Every other declared return type reports its own mismatch
// ("return type mismatch: expected i32, got void"), which names what the annotation asked
// for — a better message, so this gate stays out of its way.
//
// A VERDICT pin: the program fails to build either way; the fix makes it fail at the check
// tier, which no `@log` can express.
function pv() { print(1) }
function fwd(): void { return pv() }
fwd()
